  "body": "The motivation for this change is that previously:\n\n> If node is formatting element, then break.\n\ncould easily be misunderstood as \"if node is a formatting element\" (i.e. any element in the formatting category), instead of referring to the \"formatting element\" variable. While the source file does declare the variables with the `<var>` tag, in the rendered spec this just becomes italic text, which is easy to miss.\n\nThis commit therefore changes all the variables in the adoption agency algorithm to use camelCase, to prevent such possible confusion.\n\nTrivia: camelCase is apparently the more modern style in WHATWG specs. Before this change the spec contained ~1,500 var elements containing a space and ~5,000 var elements using camelCase, as reported by:\n\n    [...document.querySelectorAll('var')].reduce(\n      ([spaceCase, camelCase], v) => [\n        spaceCase + v.textContent.trim().includes(' '),\n        camelCase + (v.textContent != v.textContent.toLowerCase()),\n      ],\n      [0, 0]\n    );\n\n\n<!--\n    This comment and the below content is programmatically generated.\n    You may add a comma-separated list of anchors you'd like a\n    direct link to below (e.g. #idl-serializers, #idl-sequence):\n\n    Don't remove this comment or modify anything below this line.\n    If you don't want a preview generated for this pull request,\n    just replace the whole of this comment's content by \"no preview\"\n    and remove what's below.\n-->\n***\n<a href=\"https://whatpr.org/html/9786/parsing.html\" title=\"Last updated on Sep 26, 2023, 2:18 AM UTC (806f105)\">/parsing.html</a>  ( <a href=\"https://whatpr.org/html/9786/4d5e832...806f105/parsing.html\" title=\"Last updated on Sep 26, 2023, 2:18 AM UTC (806f105)\">diff</a> )",
